# Control Plane configuration (login, keys, scheduler).
# Copy to deploy/local/oauth.env (git-ignored) and fill in. run-dev.sh auto-sources it.
#
# ★ The GIT PROVIDERS' OAuth apps are NOT configured here any more (docs/log/71). GitHub's
# and Bitbucket's "connect with OAuth" apps are per-tenant rows, registered in the
# Console under Tenant settings -> Integrations -> Git provider OAuth, and env is not
# read for them at all — not even as a fallback. GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID /
# BITBUCKET_OAUTH_KEY / BITBUCKET_OAUTH_SECRET set here have no effect on the git
# connect buttons; the first of those still means the GitHub SIGN-IN app (below).
#
# On a single-user run (AUTH=dev, which deploy/native/af pins) the fixed user is a
# super_admin, so that screen is reachable with no further setup.

# External base URL the Console is served at (used to build the login and the
# Bitbucket connect redirect_uri). With CP-native OAuth the CP is served at the Funnel
# root, so this is the bare host (no /agent-fleet path).
PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://af.example.com

# --- AUTH=oauth (CP-native login; replaces oauth2-proxy + Caddy) ---
# The Control Plane sits directly behind Tailscale Funnel and runs the login
# itself, carrying the email in a signed session cookie. Set AUTH=oauth and bind
# the CP to loopback (Funnel proxies to it locally). docs: ~/docs/funnel-auth-setup.md
#   AUTH=oauth
#   CP_ADDR=127.0.0.1:8099
#
# Google OAuth 2.0 Client (Web application). Reuse the existing oauth2-proxy
# client: its Authorized redirect URI https://<host>/oauth2/callback already
# matches CP-native OAuth at the root.
#   GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=<client-id>.apps.googleusercontent.com
#   GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=<client-secret>
#
# Session cookie HMAC key. Generate once:  head -c 32 /dev/urandom | base64
#   AF_COOKIE_SECRET=<base64-32-bytes>
#   AF_SESSION_TTL=168h                   # optional; default 168h (7 days)
#
# Any OIDC IdP instead of (or alongside) Google — Entra ID / Okta / Keycloak /
# Auth0 / Cognito / GitLab all run on the same generic client (docs/log/61). The
# redirect URI stays the single https://<host>/oauth2/callback. The login page
# grows one button per enabled provider.
#   AF_OIDC_PROVIDERS=entra
#   AF_OIDC_ENTRA_ISSUER=https://login.microsoftonline.com/<tenant-guid>/v2.0
#   AF_OIDC_ENTRA_CLIENT_ID=<application-client-id>
#   AF_OIDC_ENTRA_CLIENT_SECRET=<client-secret-value>
#   AF_OIDC_ENTRA_TRUST=issuer          # REQUIRED: email_verified | issuer
#   AF_OIDC_ENTRA_LABEL_JA=Microsoft でサインイン
#   AF_OIDC_ENTRA_LABEL_EN=Sign in with Microsoft
#   #AF_OIDC_ENTRA_ALLOWED_DOMAINS=     # per-provider list; unset = the shared one
#   #AF_OIDC_ENTRA_ALLOWED_TIDS=        # REQUIRED only for a /common/ issuer
# ★ Pin the issuer to your tenant guid. With the /common/ or /organizations/
#   endpoint every Microsoft account in the world reaches the login and personal
#   accounts can rewrite their own email, so the CP refuses to start there unless
#   ALLOWED_TIDS is set.
# ★ TRUST has no default on purpose: it states why this IdP's email may be
#   believed. Entra never emits email_verified => use issuer (tenant is pinned).
# A local Keycloak over plain http is accepted on loopback only:
#   AF_OIDC_KC_ISSUER=http://localhost:8080/realms/dev
#
# GitHub as a sign-in button (docs/log/61 §61.7). Not OIDC, so it has its own knobs.
# Membership in one of AF_GITHUB_ALLOWED_ORGS is what authorizes the sign-in, and
# setting that list is also what ENABLES this button.
#   AF_GITHUB_ALLOWED_ORGS=acme,acme-labs
#   GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=<client-id>
#   GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=<client-secret>
# ★ Since docs/log/71 this pair is ONLY about signing in — the git connect flow reads the
#   tenant's own app from the database, so the two no longer share an app by default.
#   Add the callback URL $PUBLIC_BASE_URL/oauth2/callback to this OAuth App.
#   AF_GITHUB_LOGIN_CLIENT_ID / _SECRET still override it if you want the login on a
#   separate registration.
# ★ Strongly recommended: also gate on your company domain. GitHub hands us the
#   account's PRIMARY VERIFIED address, which for most people is a personal one —
#   and a personal address means a NEW, empty workspace rather than theirs, since
#   accounts under different addresses are never merged (docs/log/61 §61.5).
#   AF_GITHUB_ALLOWED_DOMAINS=example.co.jp
#   #AF_GITHUB_ALLOWED_EMAILS=
#   #AF_GITHUB_LABEL_JA=GitHub でサインイン
#   #AF_GITHUB_LABEL_EN=Sign in with GitHub
# Org membership costs an API call, so a positive answer is cached per person and
# refreshed after the TTL; if GitHub is down, the last positive answer stands for
# the grace window and is then refused.
#   #AF_GITHUB_MEMBERSHIP_TTL=10m
#   #AF_GITHUB_MEMBERSHIP_GRACE=1h
# ★ If your org restricts third-party OAuth apps, an org owner must APPROVE this
#   app — until then the membership check sees nothing and everyone is rejected.
#
# Allowlist (successor to oauth2-proxy emails.txt). Combine any of the three.
# Since docs/log/61 P3 this is not the only door: somebody INVITED to a tenant in the
# Admin panel gets in without being listed here, and a tenant's auto_join_domains
# admits a whole domain. Fail closed still holds — with no list, no member and no
# auto-join domain, every login is denied.
#   AF_OAUTH_ALLOWED_EMAILS=you@example.com,teammate@example.com   # exact emails
#   AF_OAUTH_ALLOWED_DOMAINS=example.com,foo.co.jp                   # whole domains
# The file is re-read on each login (edits need no restart). One entry per line:
# an exact email, or "@example.com" for a whole domain; # comments allowed.
# Use an ABSOLUTE path (the CP's working dir is not guaranteed to be the repo root).
#   AF_OAUTH_ALLOWED_EMAILS_FILE=/home/k1/workspace-private/agent-fleet/deploy/local/allowed-emails.txt

# --- shared form via external gateway (AUTH=proxy) ---
# Resolves the real user from an upstream oauth2-proxy email header instead of
# CP-native OAuth; each user then gets their own Workspace (af-ws-<user>). Leave
# unset (or AUTH=dev) for the single fixed-user dev form. In proxy mode a request
# with no gateway header is denied (401), so bind the CP to loopback.
#   AUTH=proxy
#   CP_ADDR=127.0.0.1:8099
#   AUTH_EMAIL_HEADER=X-Forwarded-Email   # default; override if the gateway differs
#   DEV_USER=dev                          # fixed user id when AUTH=dev

# At-rest credential encryption (A3). Master key for sealing each user's stored
# tokens; per-user subkeys are derived from it (HMAC) and injected into the
# container. Keep this OUT of the data dir (it lives here, not under WS_DATA) so
# a snapshot of the workspaces is ciphertext-only. Generate once:
#   head -c 32 /dev/urandom | base64
# Unset => no encryption (dev: Agent stores secrets as plaintext JSON).
#   AF_MASTER_KEY=<base64-32-bytes>

# --- scheduled execution (docs/log/38) ---
# The CP scheduler that fires operator-authored schedules (cron/interval/once) on a
# wall-clock: it wakes a stopped workspace, injects the prompt, and reports back. ON by
# default with a 1m tick (unset = 1m); it is a no-op while no schedule exists. Set 0 to
# hard-disable it (no timed wakes), which also hides the Console schedules section.
#   AF_SCHEDULER_INTERVAL=1m       # tick cadence; unset = 1m (on). 0 = hard-off
#   AF_SCHEDULE_JITTER=2m          # deterministic per-schedule fire spread; 0 = exact slot
#   AF_SCHEDULE_SETTLE=5m          # keep a woken workspace up this long after a fire
#   AF_SCHEDULE_WAKE_TIMEOUT=300s  # how long a fire waits for the woken agent (whole cold boot)
